# panel-exceptions.psv -- programs whose panel is RIGHT as it stands, though
# tools/audit_panels.py would score it as failing.  name | why
#
# An exception is a debt with a reason attached.  Every line here should be
# one that a stranger reading the card would agree with; the moment a
# better card is possible, the line comes out.
perr|perr prints the text of an OS-9 error number; error text IS its output
elvprsv|run BY elvis when it dies, to preserve the buffer -- `PRESERVE' in elvis's own config.h is the NAME OF THIS PROGRAM, which is how elvis calls it. By hand with nothing to preserve it prints nothing and the card says so. Measured 2026-09-21: it also forks a module called `shell' to send the notification mail (`mail "-s=%s preserved!" %s' is in the binary), so on a system without one the mail half cannot run either. Not the same pair as `expreserve'/`exrecover' -- those are ex's, and use a different directory; see the exrecover line
qp|expands back-quotes in a command line: it forks the quoted command into /PIPE, reads the output back, and chains the expanded line onward. ESTABLISHED 2026-09-21, and it is an OPTION SYNTAX mismatch, not the pipe. SRC/eff_qp/qp.c takes its shell from $SHELL, falling back to `zsh' (EFFO's shell, not on this disk), and passes it the option string `-ny -nl' -- lines 6, 7 and 175 to 179. SYS/login sets SHELL=/dd/CMDS/ksh, which does not take those options, so both the fork and the chain do nothing and qp returns 0 in silence. Proved by pointing SHELL at a program that reports its arguments: `export SHELL=/dd/CMDS/ls; qp dir `whoami`' answers "ls: invalid option -- y" TWICE, once for each. So it reaches the shell and the shell is the wrong one. MICROWARE'S SHELL IS WRONG TOO -- measured 2026-09-22 with /h1/CMDS/shell loaded and SHELL=shell: it echoes ` -ny -nl echo su' and answers `syntax error'. Those are options of EFFO's zsh, which qp was written for and which no archive here carries, so no shell a reader has will do; `run', `su', `if', `ff', `xc', `mshell' and `clock' all work with Microware's shell loaded and qp does not
checkenv|prints its syntax line on every call and answers only in its status; the card shows both statuses
splman|the spooler manager, and it RUNS -- it does not block on a device, which is what the reason here used to say. Measured 2026-09-20 with os9exec's `iprocs': `splman &' leaves a process asleep in F$Sleep at priority 126, which is a polling daemon doing its job on an empty queue. It says nothing by design, as DOC/STATUS has recorded since 2026-08-20, so there is nothing for a card to show but that it started
splprt|the spooler's printer process, and it does NOT return -- measured 2026-09-20, `splprt &' leaves a process asleep in F$Sleep, the same polling daemon shape as its manager `splman'. There is no printer device here for it to print to (os9exec maps /p, /p1 and /lp* to a printer file manager, but the implementation is Windows-only: on macOS and linux an open answers E_MNF 221), and a daemon that says nothing by design gives a card nothing to show
disktest|measures disk performance and does not come back here, so its syntax is what there is to show and the card says so. WHY, established 2026-09-19 with the os9exec session: it asks OS-9's own `free' for the sector count through a pipe and reads that pipe until the answer comes. It holds a write-capable path to the same pipe itself, so with no `free' to answer, the pipe never reaches EOF and it waits on itself at 0% CPU. Where `free' is to hand and the directory is on an RBF device it runs to the end -- and prints NEGATIVE rates, from its own int overflow on a 33 MB buffer. It is not destructive: the only file it writes is ./disktest.tmp, which it unlinks
trap|an example trap handler that cannot be installed from user state; the refusal is the whole of what it does here, and the card says so
puzzle|a G-Windows program: at a terminal one line of its tile frame is all it draws, and the card says so
collect|a g++ linker-helper pass; with nothing to link its usage is what there is to show
gcc_collect|a g++ linker-helper pass; with nothing to link its usage is what there is to show
gpp_collect|a g++ linker-helper pass; with nothing to link its usage is what there is to show
gouldtoppm|reads a Gould scanner file, no sample ships; its card shows it naming what it wants
hpcdtoppm|reads a PhotoCD image, no sample ships; its card shows it naming what it wants
spottopgm|reads a SPOT satellite image, no sample ships; its card shows its usage
ximtoppm|reads an Xim image, no sample ships; its card shows it naming what it wants
ptxminst|installs the ptxm path-table module into the kernel, which needs system state not available here; its usage is what there is to show
frm|lists who your mail is from, and answers "tester has no mail." -- but the mailbox is NOT empty, which is what the reason here used to say. Measured 2026-09-20 under SYS/login: MAIL=/dd/SPOOL/MAIL/tester, 804 bytes, beginning with a proper `From os9-freeware Sat Aug 16 ...' line, and `messages' reads the same file and answers "There is 1 message in your mailbox". So frm disagrees with its neighbour about one file. Its source is not on the disk -- only DOC frm.1 -- and the cause is still not established, but THE BIFF GUESS THAT STOOD HERE IS RULED OUT. Measured 2026-09-21: copy the mailbox somewhere fresh, point MAIL at the copy and frm answers "You have no mail."; APPEND to that copy so its mtime is newer than any read of it, and frm still answers the same. Naming the folder on the command line -- `frm /dd/SPOOL/MAIL/tester', with -v and with -n -- gives "tester has no mail." every time, so it is not finding or not parsing the folder rather than judging it stale. The folder itself is well formed: it opens `From os9-freeware Sat Aug 16 00:00:00 1986' and `messages' counts one message in it. FIVE THINGS ARE NOW RULED OUT, all measured 2026-09-21: staleness (append to the folder and it still says none), location (the folder put at MAIL/tester, at SPOOL/MAIL/tester and at a path of its own), naming it on the command line (with -v and -n), the message's age (a folder built fresh with a 2026 date), and the line ending (an LF-terminated folder and a CR-terminated one, both on a host directory, both refused). Note WHICH refusal it gives: with MAIL set it says "You have no mail." and without it "tester has no mail." -- so it OPENS what MAIL names and counts nothing in it. `messages' reads the same file through the same MAIL and finds the message. It is frm's own parsing, and settling it needs the source, which is not here.
smail|the smart mail router; it needs a routing configuration and a peer to hand mail to, neither present, so its options are what there is to show
uucico|the UUCP transfer program; it dials or answers a UUCP peer over a serial line, of which there is none here, so its options are what there is to show
uulog|shows the UUCP transfer log, AND IT WORKS -- the reason that stood here, that nothing has been logged so there is nothing to print, was contradicted by its own card, which had a log line on it. That line came from the `rmail' stanza 660 lines earlier in the same sheet, which is the cross-card dependency the gate exists to prevent. The card makes its own entry now: it clears the log, runs `rmail tester' (which attempts a local delivery, does not find the mailbox in the shape it wants, and logs the failure) and then `uulog', which prints it back with the site, the time and the reason. Two shoots in a row give one entry, not two. It stays excepted only because BOTH visible lines contain the word error -- rmail's own and the log's copy of it -- so audit_panels scores the card mostly-error. Reporting a logged error IS this program's job
msntp|sets the system clock from a network time server, and its card names one so that what shows is where it stops: the address lookup. Traced 2026-09-23 on os9exec b5da6df with -d 0x0602: it tries to load a module `netdb' (E$PNNF), then prints `unable to locate IP address/number' -- for 127.0.0.1 as readily as for a name. The first thing it stops on is Microware's netdb module, which the reader's own networking supplies; what it would meet past that is not measured -- its binary names /ip0#1/udp0 and its siblings where ttcp, which does reach the host network through os9exec's /socket, names /socket. Named none it listens for broadcasts and waits, which DOC/msntp/msntp.1 describes -- correct behaviour, not a fault
nslookup|looks names up on a name server; its card gives it a resolv.conf naming 127.0.0.1, where nothing answers, so the lines saying the server is not available are the program's own report. Re-shot 2026-09-23 on os9exec a4b338e, whose /socket carries its query.
blastem|XMODEM and YMODEM file transfer for the MM/1; it needs a modem on a serial path, and its help is what there is to show without one
disable|turns MTSMon off on a serial port; it needs the port's driver module resident to find it, and none is loaded
enable|the counterpart to disable on a serial port; it too needs the port's driver module resident, and none is loaded
setfont|loads a downloadable terminal font into a terminal that can receive one. THE DISK DOES SHIP FONTS FOR IT -- six 768-byte files in DOC/wolk -- so the old reason here, that it "prints its usage without one", was answering a question nobody had asked it. Measured 2026-09-20 with DOC/wolk/std.us.font: it returns status 0 and writes NO byte, to the screen or anywhere else, which is what DOC/INDEX has recorded all along. It opens /term and the font goes to the terminal hardware; there is nothing for a card to show either way
sbreak|sends a break signal on a serial device; there is no serial device here to send one on, so it reports that a device must be given
authwn|WN's authenticator for a password-protected area; inetd and wn invoke it per request, and run alone it has nothing to authenticate
inetdc|what inetd forks to handle each connection; run by hand with nothing to serve it exits in silence
basicwin|an X11 basic-window demo; with no X server here it stops at "cannot connect to X server"
xengine|an X11 demo animating an engine; with no X display here it stops at "Can't open display"
snap|writes a snapshot of the screen to a file, and it does that by READING THE SCREEN BACK OUT OF THE TERMINAL -- it positions the cursor and reads each line as the terminal reports it, which is a facility of the terminal and not of the program. Two are supported, from the table in SRC/snap/main.c: a Kimtron KT7 (TERM kt7, amb85h, abm85) and a Wyse 50 (wy50, wyse50, wy50-t, wyse50-t); anything else gets "Unknown terminal type". No VT100 or VT220 can answer that, which is every console here, and neither terminal is in SYS/termcap either. Re-read 2026-09-20 and the reason holds -- what is new is that it is the terminal's read-back that is missing, not a recognition table we could widen
transfer|copies files from GDOS disks to OS-9; it wants the GDOS device DGDOS0, which is not present
mvolformat|formats a set of floppies with sequential volume numbers; there is no floppy drive here, so its syntax is what there is to show
dpark|restores an RBF disk's head to track zero; there is no disk head to park here, so it answers with its command format
setime|sets the system clock, and the prompt -- `YYMMDDHHMMSS' -- is all a card can show, because NOTHING HERE CAN MOVE THE CLOCK. ESTABLISHED 2026-09-21 and it is not this program: os9exec's F$STime is a no-op off classic MacOS -- the source says so in as many words, "the host clock is not set from the guest" -- and it RETURNS SUCCESS, so every clock-setter on this disk reports the time and leaves it alone. Measured on all four: `setimex -s=20260925120000' and `setyear 2030' both print a date and `getsys' still reads the host's day afterwards; `setime2' prompts. The line that stood here said `setimex' and `setyear' DO set the clock and have cards that show it -- they do not, and their cards never showed it; all three captions are corrected. On a real system these programs work
rndir|renames subdirectory names to upper or lower case, and does nothing at all here. Measured 2026-09-20 with /r0 mounted: on `Notes'/`Papers' and again on `ALPHA'/`BETA', with -l, with -l -d and with -l -n -d, it exits 0, prints nothing, renames nothing and leaves no temporary anywhere. The reason recorded before -- that a case-insensitive filesystem makes a name and its other-case form one entry, so there is nothing to change -- does NOT hold: RBF is case-insensitive for LOOKUP but keeps the case it was given, so ALPHA becoming alpha would show in ls. Cause not established; no source on the disk.
fixyear|corrects a file's modification year when the clock wrote a wrong one -- specifically any year earlier than 1970 -- and the files here carry good years, so it finds nothing to fix. THE OBVIOUS WAY TO MAKE IT ONE CANNOT WORK, and the reason is established rather than observed: os9exec's F$STime is a no-op off classic MacOS -- its source says "the host clock is not set from the guest" -- and it returns SUCCESS, so no program here can move the clock back. Measured before the source was read: `setimex -s=19650704120000' and `setyear 2030' both print a date and leave `getsys' and `dir -e' reading the host's. Without a clock that can be moved there is no way to write a file with a pre-1970 year for fixyear to repair. On a real system, both halves work
ppmtorgb3|writes three RGB plane files silently; the ls that follows on its card, three 1165-byte files, is the evidence
lfmaker|builds a G-Windows launch file, and before anything else asks its terminal for setstat $4749, a G-Windows call; told E$UnkSvc it stops in silence with status 208 (DOC/STATUS traced it 2026-09-19). Measured again 2026-09-23 on os9exec b5da6df and a4b338e alike: `lfmaker' and `lfmaker readme' both print nothing and return 208, run bare first or not. The No-more-memory request this reason used to describe is gone -- it was os9exec's old 512-block ceiling -- so on a terminal without G-Windows silence is what there is to show
xy|waiting to receive on the terminal, since no MODEM port is set: the `C' it sends to ask for a CRC transfer is the whole of what a receiver shows until a sender answers, and the card says so
k|waiting for the first Kermit packet on the terminal: `Receiving...' is all a receiver shows until a sender answers, and the card says so
xyt|it wants a MODEM port and there is none to give it; saying so is what it does here, and the card says so
tterm|it wants a MODEM port and there is none to give it; saying so is what it does here, and the card says so
infoxpress|an InfoXpress network client; it opens a serial device to reach its host and there is none here, so it returns without printing anything at all
filter|elm's mail filter. The recorded reason -- that /r0 is a device this disk has not got -- is WRONG TWICE, measured 2026-09-20. `mount -r=256k /r0' makes one from bash in one line, as DOC/README-RUNNING has documented since August, and it works (write, list, read back). With it mounted filter reads its rules, MATCHES them and says "Message saved in folder" -- and then fails with "can't open temp file /r0/filter.26048 for reading!" because it WROTE /r0/filter.3. The cause is in the port: actions.c wraps `filter_pid = getpid()' inside `#ifndef OSK' with the fork-for-group code, so on OSK save_to_folder builds the temp name from an uninitialised variable. One line, patched in SRC and NOT in the shipped binary -- building filter wants ELM's libutil.l and osk.l and this tree has no lib/.
cpucache|switches a 68020-68040's on-chip caches, which the emulator has not got, through a driver this disk does not carry; its usage is what can be shown, and the card says it is untested
errlog|an error-logging daemon for a multi-user system; its usage is what can be shown here, and the card says so
erradm|drives errlog's log; its usage is what can be shown here, and the card says so
logon|logs a user in on a multi-user system with terminal lines; its usage is what can be shown here, and the card says it is untested
timeout|logon's idle timer, which logon starts; it prints nothing by hand
mmon|a timesharing monitor for a terminal or modem line; its usage is what can be shown here, and the card says it is untested
rex|runs a command on another OS-9/Net node; without a network its usage and its refusal are what can be shown, and the card says so
chp|a remote login over OS-9/Net; without a network there is nothing for it to reach, and the rex card says so
remdate|reads the clock of another OS-9/Net node; without a network it can only say it cannot open /n0
prexd|the OS-9/Net daemon rex talks to; without a network it has nothing to serve
prexdc|the server prexd starts for each request; not run by hand
vt100|a terminal program for the MM/1; with no serial port here its refusal is what can be shown, and the card says it is untested
x9eyes|draws in the Sharp X68000's personal window; without the X68000 it stops at the window it cannot open, and the card says so
setbgptn|sets the Sharp X68000 personal window's background; without the X68000 it can only say it cannot open /win
gcc137_cccp|a pass of GNU C 1.37.1 that the driver runs, not a command; the gcc137 card shows the driver running it
gcc137_cc1|a pass of GNU C 1.37.1 that the driver runs, not a command; the gcc137 card shows the driver running it and the assembly it wrote
gcc272_cccp2|a pass of GNU C 2.7.2 that the driver runs, not a command; the gcc272 card shows the driver running it
gcc272_cc2|a pass of GNU C 2.7.2 that the driver runs, not a command; the gcc272 card shows the driver running it and the assembly it wrote
gcc272_cc2plus|the C++ pass of GNU C 2.7.2, which the driver runs for a .cc file; not a command
gcc272_collect|the pass GNU C 2.7.2 runs at every link to gather C++ constructors; not a command
mesg|sets your flags in SYS/utmp, which logon writes on a multi-user system; with nobody logged in its usage is what can be shown, and the card says so
msg|sends a line to a logged-in user and prints nothing of its own; mmenu runs it, and the mesg card says so
speak|a chat with another logged-in user; with nobody else logged in its refusal is what can be shown, and the card says so
newgrp|changes your group as SYS/group allows, and this disk carries no SYS/group; its usage is what can be shown
mmenu|a menu system read from menu files a multi-user site writes for itself; with none here its usage is what can be shown
mwb|edits a manual directory under USR/DOC/.MAN that a site keeps; with none here its usage is what can be shown
